INT. ORBITAL HYDROPONICS RING - NIGHT CYCLE

Rows of green under violet LEDs. Nutrient mist. Tomatoes on vines the length of a bus. Everything is damp and humming.

KESTREL ODUYA, 30s, a botanist in a stained lab coat, pinches leaves between her fingers. Cuts a sample. Bags it. IXCHEL TORRES-WAI, 20s, her junior, trails with a tablet, scribbling. BRAM VANDERHOEK, 40s, a station tech, jogs down the central aisle, wrench in hand.

BRAM
Kes. The mist valves are fluctuating.

KESTREL
How badly?

BRAM
Row twelve is flooding.

She runs. Ixchel follows. The mist thickens as they go, and the LEDs flicker, a violet strobe.

Row twelve. A tomato plant slumps, roots exposed, water pooling at its base. Bram kneels, jams the wrench under the manifold. He grunts. Pulls.

The pipe bursts. Water jets across the aisle. Ixchel yelps. Kestrel ducks. Bram swears and falls backward onto the deck, drenched.

KESTREL
Shut it off!

BRAM
I'm trying!

They scramble. Kestrel finds the valve. She yanks it. The spray dies. The three of them stand soaked and breathless.

OMAR EL-SAYED, 40s, a surveyor in a grey jumpsuit, appears at the end of the row, a sealed data case in one hand and a badge on a lanyard.

He doesn't smile. He holds out the badge.

OMAR
Station Authority. Section audit.

KESTREL
Tonight?

OMAR
Tonight.

He walks down the row, stepping over puddles. His eyes find the broken pipe. The slumped plant. The sample bag. Kestrel steps in front of it.

BRAM
(whispering to Ixchel)
That's not on the schedule.

IXCHEL
(whispering back)
It never is.

OMAR
Dr. Oduya. You've been logging Row twelve as nominal for six months.

KESTREL
Because it was.

OMAR
Your yield numbers say otherwise.

He opens the case. A screen flares to life. Columns of numbers, red.

Ixchel grabs Bram's sleeve. Bram sets down his wrench. Slowly, Kestrel picks up the sample bag and holds it behind her back.

KESTREL
The plant's mutated.

OMAR
I know.

Silence. The mist hisses.

OMAR (CONT'D)
So does the Council. They sent me to see if it's dangerous.

KESTREL
And?

He crouches. Touches a root. It wriggles. All four step back at once.

OMAR
(softly)
Doctor. Is it supposed to do that?

The root coils around his finger.

Ixchel screams. Bram lunges. Kestrel grabs her scalpel. Omar yanks back, but the root holds, and the plant lifts, slowly, from the soil, a green fist rising out of the dark.

KESTREL
Everybody out. Now.

They run, boots slapping through water, the violet lights flashing behind them, the plant unfolding into the aisle like a waking god.
